# a-wreck-fetched-under-fire — plan 15 C3, and the answer is a bet the
# base does not live long enough to settle.
#
# `a-wreck-left-where-it-fell.keys` with the retrieval in it and nothing
# else changed: the same band, the same gate, the same wall, the same
# wave of eight, the same crew member lost in the same hex.  The only
# difference is that the player drives out, picks the wreck up, carries
# it to the core and delivers it.
#
#   nobody goes back for it        85 ticks
#   this file — fetched            (see the band below)
#
# ⚠ **The crew member never comes back**, and that is the finding rather
# than a bug.  Recovery is 60 s — exactly 90 ticks — and the base falls
# about 85 ticks after the wave arrives, so the clock the delivery
# starts is longer than the base has left.  Delivering is the whole
# mechanic working correctly; there is simply no wave left for the
# returning crew member to meet.
#
# ⚠ **And the reason is a feature that is missing, not a number that is
# wrong.**  `numbers.json` § wave_system prices a base at SEVEN waves
# with a 15 s lull between them, which is where a 60 s recovery belongs:
# lose somebody on wave 2, get them back for wave 5.  dryopea plays ONE
# wave at a time — `wave <n>` is authored by the script and there is no
# wave system driving a list — so the base ends when the first wave gets
# in.  ⚠ The trigger for re-measuring this file is that wave system, not
# a tune of `helper.recovery_time_after_retrieval`.
#
# ⚠ What the fetch DOES buy is the drive itself: the player standing in
# the gate is a blocker too, so the wave stops against it for as long as
# it is there.  That is the whole difference between the two clocks, and
# it is the player's own body rather than the crew member's return.

echo a wreck fetched under fire: delivered, and the base falls before the clock runs out

do toggle_mode

do cycle_kind
click 0 0
marker 0 0 target

do cycle_kind
do cycle_kind
do rotate
do rotate
do rotate
click 18 0
marker 18 0 spawn 3

count markers 2 2
do toggle_mode

palette 5
drag -3 -2 20 -2
drag -3 -1 20 -1
drag -3 0 20 0
drag -3 1 20 1
drag -3 2 20 2
count painted 120 120

palette 9
click 6 -2
click 6 -1
click 6 1
click 6 2
kind 6 0 grass
kind 6 1 wall

crew 6 0
helper 0 6 0
roster 1 1
park 0 0

wave 8
count alive 8 8
wallet 200.0 200.0

# ── The loss ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# Twelve hexes of walking and ten seconds of blocking at 5 HP/s.
tick 38
roster 0 0
cargo 1 1

# ── The fetch, into the hex the wave is coming through ──────────
#
# ⚠ This is the design test `CLAUDE.md` § What dryopea is applies to
# every mechanic: using it costs something, and the cost here is not
# invented.  The wreck lies in the gate, so going to get it means
# parking in the one hex the wave has to use — and the player takes
# blocker damage for every tick it stands there.
drive 6 0
tick 3
vehicle 6 0

take player
cargo 1 1

drive 0 0
tick 3
vehicle 0 0

drop player
cargo 0 0

# The recovery clock has started, and the base will not outlive it.
roster 0 0

fall 800
ticks 82 92

# ⚠ **Still nobody**, 90 ticks of recovery never having finished.
roster 0 0

snap c3_a_wreck_fetched_under_fire
